  "worked_solution": "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =10.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
